From 65ef6b232457deed474feed0e2084d00980335ff Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: z060142 <z060142@yahoo.com.tw>
Date: Sun, 13 Sep 2026 00:34:30 +0800
Subject: [PATCH 221/221] fix(area): road component builds the stock road
 sectors

The road followed the spline but was not the road CRoadObject makes. The cause
was the walk, not the render node.

CRoadObject walks the curve SEGMENT BY SEGMENT (SetRoadSectors,
RoadObject.cpp:158-231): each Bezier segment is asked for its length, that length
divided by the step gives int((length + 0.5) / step) sectors for THAT segment -
never fewer than one - and position, normal and width are then all evaluated at
the same segment-local parameter. The component walked by arc length instead and,
worse, took the position from PosByDistance() while taking normal and width at
t = distance / totalLength. On a Bezier curve those are two different points, so
every station's edges were built from a frame belonging to somewhere else: the
ribbon sheared wherever the control points were unevenly spaced, and the sector
boundaries fell in places legacy would never put them.

SplineSectors.h now holds that walk once, against the shape contract, and both the
road and the river use it - which is exactly right, because CRiverObject IS a
CRoadObject and inherits the sector generation verbatim (RiverObject.cpp:195-230).
ISplineShape gained the three append-only queries it needs - GetSegmentCount,
GetSegmentLength(index, segmentT) and SegmentToParam - with defaults that make a
kind which cannot answer fall back to a parameter-uniform walk rather than to the
mismatched one. GetSegmentLength is CSplineObject::GetBezierSegmentLength's
32-chord approximation, deliberately the same coarseness, so sector counts and
texture coordinates come out where legacy puts them.

Also aligned with CRoadObject::UpdateSectors (:283-326):

- texture coordinates per chunk are now the stations' own arc lengths divided by
  Tile Length (CRoadSector::t0, :195), not a uniform step, and the global range is
  the first and last station's, which is what the end fade reads;
- node setup order is flags, view distance ratio, min spec, material layers,
  editor id, and only then SetVertices - which is what schedules the rebuild that
  turns them into a mesh. Min spec and material layers were not pushed at all.

Unchanged because they already matched: vertex order (left = +normal first, which
is what GetClipPlanes assumes when it builds a trapezoid's clip planes,
RoadRenderNode.cpp:647-650), 16 sectors per node, the 7.5 cm end overlap, the
defaults, material handling (CMaterial::AssignToEntity is SetMaterial and nothing
else, Material.cpp:1331-1335), and registration (Compile ends with RegisterEntity,
RoadRenderNode.cpp:462).

